comments.body:
@reporter glad to hear you are pushing #36452 forward. Happy to help test or review the PR once it is up — we have been running verification gates in production for a while so can share what edge cases come up.

One thing we found: the gate needs to handle partial matches gracefully. A claim might be "partially supported" (right entity, wrong number) which is different from fully contradicted. Our SDK returns per-claim verdicts with confidence scores rather than a binary pass/fail, which gives the downstream chain more to work with.

If it would be useful, `langchain-veroq` on PyPI has a working reference implementation you can look at for the gate interface.

@reporter the physical layer integrity angle is interesting — hadn't thought about it from that perspective. Our approach is more at the semantic layer: does the content of the output match reality, regardless of how it was produced.

The two layers complement each other well. Your protocol handles "was this output tampered with in transit" and ours handles "was this output factually correct when produced." Different failure modes, both matter in production.

For the LangChain PR, one thing that would make the gate more useful: exposing the raw claim-level results (not just pass/fail) so downstream chains can make nuanced decisions. A chain might want to pass through "partially supported" claims but block "contradicted" ones. That granularity is what makes it practical vs theoretical.

@reporter nice — will check out the trustflow_agent.py and VERIFICATION.md. Good to have the raw evidence and integrity scores already in place, that maps cleanly to what we return from verify_output.

My take on the core vs integration question: the gate interface (input schema, output schema, how it plugs into a chain) belongs in core. The specific verification implementation (VeroQ, your ChaKou Protocol, or any other provider) belongs in integration packages. That way any verification backend can plug in, and LangChain users pick the one that fits their stack.

Will review #36452 and put together a concrete interface proposal this week.
